WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.962
Path traversal prevention is about controlling how a Python application
decides which files and directories it may access.

2
00:00:06.962 --> 00:00:12.863
The risk appears when untrusted input influences a file operation in a way
the application did not intend.

3
00:00:12.863 --> 00:00:24.533
That can happen in web applications, APIs, command-line tools, upload
systems, download endpoints, report generators, static file handlers,
background workers, data pipelines, and maintenance scripts.

4
00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:30.898
Any code path that reads, writes, copies, deletes, extracts, or serves
files has a filesystem security boundary.

5
00:00:30.898 --> 00:00:37.993
File access is not the same as authorization, storage policy, or operating
system permissions, although all of them interact.

6
00:00:37.993 --> 00:00:41.308
A user may be allowed to download one report but not another.

7
00:00:41.308 --> 00:00:47.408
A process may have operating system permission to read a directory that
the application should never expose.

8
00:00:47.408 --> 00:00:55.166
A framework helper may serve files safely when used as intended, while
direct file reads can bypass the policy the team thought was in place.

9
00:00:55.166 --> 00:00:57.884
Secure design needs all of these layers to agree.

10
00:00:57.884 --> 00:01:00.669
The damage from unsafe file access can be serious.

11
00:01:00.669 --> 00:01:14.196
Depending on the application and deployment, unintended file access may
expose configuration data, source code, logs, uploaded user files,
generated reports, database backups, internal artifacts, credentials, or
application secrets.

12
00:01:14.196 --> 00:01:19.102
It may also allow writes to locations that change application behavior or
corrupt data.

13
00:01:19.102 --> 00:01:26.263
The exact impact depends on the environment, which is why prevention
should not rely on guessing which files might be sensitive.

14
00:01:26.263 --> 00:01:31.766
The strongest prevention starts with design: do not let users directly
control filesystem paths.

15
00:01:31.766 --> 00:01:38.861
Let users select objects, documents, reports, or display names, while the
application controls storage names and locations.

16
00:01:38.861 --> 00:01:43.105
Safe file access should be explicit, constrained, reviewable, and tested.

17
00:01:43.105 --> 00:01:52.520
The goal is predictable file behavior where every access is authorized,
contained inside approved locations, and understandable during review or
incident response.
